WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.243
In the previous video, we explained how Project Kamp 
works beyond the projects we normally show in our videos.

00:00:06.243 --> 00:00:10.400
One of the most interesting&nbsp;topics
 is how we manage with food.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.108
In Season 1, we set up the kitchen in a container.

00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:16.133
In Season 2, we had some people coming to cook.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:20.610
They started to organize our supplies.
 and&nbsp;to rethink our way of eating.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.894
And now, in Season 3, we have two cooks in the Kamp.

00:00:22.894 --> 00:00:26.871
We are working&nbsp;towards a vision of 
what could be a more sustainable kitchen.

00:00:26.871 --> 00:00:29.616
Welcome to a new Project&nbsp;Kamp update.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.236
Welcome to the Project Kamp kitchen!

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.444
So, this year, we are with a lot of people at the Kamp.

00:00:39.444 --> 00:00:43.985
At the moment, we are with about 25,
 which means there a lot of stomachs to feed!

00:00:43.985 --> 00:00:48.514
We would love to&nbsp;do it in the most sustainable way as possible!

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:50.905
But, we don't really know what that means...

00:00:50.905 --> 00:00:52.945
So, we&nbsp;started thinking and researching:

00:00:52.945 --> 00:00:55.205
How to supply the food?

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:56.928
How to process the food?

00:00:56.928 --> 00:00:59.803
How&nbsp;to cook the food in the most sustainable way?

00:00:59.803 --> 00:01:02.952
This year, we have two people at the&nbsp;kitchen.

00:01:02.952 --> 00:01:07.657
So, it gives a bit more buffer for one to cook,
and the other one to do the research.

00:01:07.657 --> 00:01:11.690
Is it by fate, or by accident?

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.748
I don't know. but we have two incredible German chefs,
 which&nbsp;is Timo and Paule.

00:01:16.748 --> 00:01:18.240
Let me introduce them to you!

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:22.993
- Hey! I'm Paule!

00:01:22.993 --> 00:01:27.540
I'm 29 years old, born and raised&nbsp;in Berlin,
 and I'm an absolute foodlover!

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:35.010
Back home, I focused mostly on teaching,
working in education, and playing football&nbsp;and spikeball.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:41.285
I came here to Project&nbsp;Kamp to develop my passion for cooking,
 while also trying to make people happy with&nbsp;that!

00:01:41.285 --> 00:01:45.162
I really like the social aspects about living in a community!

00:01:45.162 --> 00:01:47.451
But, for now, my home base is still in Berlin.

00:01:47.451 --> 00:01:53.820
When I come back, I want to positively influence my&nbsp;surroundings, 
and the things I have an impact on.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:58.258
- Hi! I'm Timo!

00:01:58.258 --> 00:02:00.245
I'm 38 years old.

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:03.817
I was born and&nbsp;I grew up in Germany,
 but I have my roots in Turkey as well.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.214
I enjoy being in nature, cooking,&nbsp;
exploring remote places with my van...

00:02:10.214 --> 00:02:13.514
And, finding out more about myself,
 through meditation and&nbsp;other means.

00:02:13.514 --> 00:02:17.002
Until last year, I was working as an IT consultant.

00:02:17.002 --> 00:02:19.429
I joined Project Kamp to see what it's like.

00:02:19.429 --> 00:02:24.600
To both work as a chef, and live with other people
 that have an interest in&nbsp;sustainability.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:31.460
While it's still unclear what I'll do next,
 I can say that I'm very happy being&nbsp;here and exploring the surroundings.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:39.540
So, now that you know our chefs, we can start the journey
 into&nbsp;finding out how to make a sustainable kitchen.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.898
- Hi! Good morning!

00:02:42.898 --> 00:02:45.908
It's seven in the morning,
and we are preparing breakfast right now.

00:02:45.908 --> 00:02:47.303
Come, I'll show you!

00:02:47.303 --> 00:02:52.380
For breakfast, we normally have&nbsp;some porridge,
 which Ale is preparing right now.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.200
Here you can see&nbsp;our toppings: a little bit of cacao, cinnamon...

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.920
You can also see a wonderful self-made&nbsp;bread!

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.540
In the back, you also see our coffee.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.040
It's not the most sustainable product, but...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.582
We try our best with most of the products we have.

00:03:14.582 --> 00:03:16.440
The coffee is still here!

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:24.328
So, right now, I'm preparing some bruschetta for the breakfast,
 because people are going&nbsp;crazy for that!

00:03:24.328 --> 00:03:30.060
It's some tomatoes, basil, and garlic,&nbsp;
mixed up with some oil and vinegar.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.200
Here, we have some nice basil,
and now I'm gonna&nbsp;slap it to release the oils.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:41.828
- Bad basil!

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.400
You decide to make bruschetta&nbsp;that everyone eats with bread,
 so now we&nbsp;are completely out of bread...

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.820
- I just&nbsp;want to make people happy you know!

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.924
I don't prepare breakfast every day!

00:04:06.924 --> 00:04:08.723
I just do&nbsp;it on Thursdays.

00:04:08.723 --> 00:04:14.281
Everyone has just one day to clean basically, and set up breakfast.

00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:18.524
Then,&nbsp;I have to cook every other day nevertheless, but yeah...

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.434
Here, we have our hot water.

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.225
Our soft coffee&nbsp;in a French press.

00:04:22.225 --> 00:04:24.233
Espresso from thermal pour flask.

00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:29.037
We have our kettle which we try to use as often as possible.

00:04:29.037 --> 00:04:33.178
Also for cooking in general, to not use that much gas.

00:04:33.178 --> 00:04:38.257
Energywise, we always&nbsp;have to check it in the morning.

00:04:38.257 --> 00:04:44.612
As you can see, we have 75% of battery,
 but we also can&nbsp;see that it's gonna be a sunny day

00:04:44.612 --> 00:04:49.276
So, we can use our toaster instead,
 because the most&nbsp;people like to toast their bread.

00:04:49.276 --> 00:04:56.940
If we don't have the energy capacity for it,
 we toast it in a&nbsp;pan with gas, but we want to use the toaster more.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:02.315
Pretty okay!

00:05:02.315 --> 00:05:08.608
The porridge, the toppings for the&nbsp;porridge, 
a lot of nice fruit we have right now, which is quite nice...

00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:16.080
more melon, our bread, some hummus, 
some bruschetta, and peanut butter and jam.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:32.047
The bread is already half gone,
 because&nbsp;people start to eat it all day long!

00:05:32.047 --> 00:05:33.471
- No, no! It's the people eating bread in the morning!

00:05:33.471 --> 00:05:35.280
I eat the same bread all the time!

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.580
- You need energy!

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.972
Now, we're going to actually have a&nbsp;look at the veggies.

00:06:01.972 --> 00:06:04.423
And, at the suppliers, which we're getting them from.

00:06:04.423 --> 00:06:06.180
So, let me take you there!

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.717
We do two shopping trips each week with this car.

00:06:14.717 --> 00:06:22.351
We still would like to incorporate that in our data, 
like taking the consumption of the&nbsp;car.

00:06:22.351 --> 00:06:29.019
Calculate what the CO2-footprint of the cargo
 is going these kilometers each week.

00:06:29.019 --> 00:06:33.848
We&nbsp;can see how much it is,
 in relation to the food we're getting.

00:06:33.848 --> 00:06:38.996
Maybe, we can change something&nbsp;around,
like reducing the number of shopping trips.

00:06:38.996 --> 00:06:46.148
We're, of course, trying to get as much food&nbsp;
from our neighboring farms and shops.

00:06:46.148 --> 00:06:50.529
It's 10 to 15 minutes in each direction,
so nothing is too&nbsp;far away.

00:06:50.529 --> 00:06:55.001
We actually have the the power 
to support some projects here locally.

00:06:55.001 --> 00:07:01.795
With our buying power&nbsp;for veggies and other food items,
 that we can get from the shops.

00:07:01.795 --> 00:07:07.595
You could say that all the all&nbsp;the people are supporting us financially...

00:07:07.595 --> 00:07:12.780
Or, just by watching the videos, 
you are also supporting&nbsp;these local communities here and shops.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:18.724
All right!

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:22.928
So, welcome to Nuno's farm!

00:07:31.445 --> 00:07:33.671
Nuno is one of our main suppliers.

00:07:33.671 --> 00:07:41.536
He grows veggies in a regenerative way,
 and this is just the land where he's growing them.

00:07:41.536 --> 00:07:42.926
There he is!

00:07:45.212 --> 00:07:46.389
- Hey! How are you?

00:07:47.489 --> 00:07:49.015
I'm good! How are you?

00:07:49.015 --> 00:07:50.295
- Yeah, I am good!

00:07:50.295 --> 00:07:53.212
So, I&nbsp;started farming four years ago.

00:07:53.212 --> 00:07:59.850
In 2019, which was just two years after the big fire,
 that happened in this&nbsp;region.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.212
It's when almost everything burned down.

00:08:02.998 --> 00:08:05.480
I do agro ecology.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:14.459
So, behind the organic certificator, 
we also do work on the system as a whole.

00:08:14.459 --> 00:08:18.097
Like, every being can come to eat.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:25.576
And then, what remains, because the soil 
produces enough for everyone, we eat and we sell.

00:08:25.576 --> 00:08:27.743
- Nuno does regenerative farming.

00:08:27.743 --> 00:08:30.337
That means he doesn't use any chemical fertilizers.

00:08:30.337 --> 00:08:34.153
He just&nbsp;follows natural processes that build fertile soil.

00:08:34.153 --> 00:08:36.464
- The nature always tends to regenerate itself.

00:08:36.464 --> 00:08:38.770
So, if you abandon the land, it will regenerate.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:45.514
We just try to do these kind of processes,
but now we select what we want to let grow.

00:08:45.514 --> 00:08:48.962
It creates more productivity, 
and more different food in the same&nbsp;space!

00:08:48.962 --> 00:08:52.095
But, it's always more work.

00:08:52.095 --> 00:08:58.965
- We've researched that, so far, it's one of the biggest factors&nbsp;
in the CO2-emissions of veggies.

00:08:58.965 --> 00:09:06.240
The type of land usage, like we have here, 
has a negative CO2-footprint, depending on the crop that is growing.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:12.949
You want one, Javi?

00:09:12.949 --> 00:09:13.449
- Okay!

00:09:18.091 --> 00:09:20.071
A nice strawberry!

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:22.860
Wow!

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:35.022
And here, altogether: the basil&nbsp;with strawberries,
 that you say are&nbsp;delicious, with onions, with kale...

00:09:35.022 --> 00:09:38.100
There&nbsp;were even spring onions here in the middle!

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:55.267
So, we have already here some things&nbsp;harvested.

00:09:55.267 --> 00:09:57.300
Let's save them from the sun.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.000
All of this is your food, and then we will get 
something that has been missing.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:42.451
Okay, the car is loaded.

00:10:42.451 --> 00:10:43.194
Thank&nbsp;you Nuno!

00:10:43.194 --> 00:10:44.469
- You're welcome!
I'll see you next week!

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:49.160
Now, we're heading to Tata.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:51.120
A zero&nbsp;waste bulkshop in a town nearby.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.307
We're in the Tata Bulkshop!

00:11:00.129 --> 00:11:01.494
We just did&nbsp;our shopping!

00:11:01.494 --> 00:11:08.134
We got some big bags of whole grain flour
 and all the white flour.

00:11:08.134 --> 00:11:09.757
We have&nbsp;some pasta, soy chunks...

00:11:09.757 --> 00:11:13.382
We usually bring some of our glasses here.

00:11:13.382 --> 00:11:20.146
Some blueberries, shiitake mushrooms,
 apple vinegar, jams that we get from here...

00:11:20.146 --> 00:11:23.744
We're very happy that this shop exists here!

00:11:23.744 --> 00:11:32.372
About the two wonderful people, who had the idea 
to found the shop, are Tiago and Daniela.

00:11:32.372 --> 00:11:39.651
- Yeah, we decided to open this zero-waste/bulkstore,
 because we wanted to really let go on plastic.

00:11:39.651 --> 00:11:46.723
Also, it creates a more sustainable living,
 and offer that to the people near us.

00:11:46.723 --> 00:11:49.929
In the main city,&nbsp;there's a like three or four now.

00:11:49.929 --> 00:11:54.184
Back then, there were zero,
and Daniela and I were still the only ones!

00:11:54.184 --> 00:11:57.977
We opened two years ago, in a month or so.

00:11:57.977 --> 00:12:00.753
We were like: ''Oh my God! Maybe this is not gonna&nbsp;work!''

00:12:00.753 --> 00:12:03.660
Fortunately, we're still here!

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.922
- The last stop of our shopping trip&nbsp;is Ecoseiva, an organic farm.

00:12:30.922 --> 00:12:35.235
We source from them as well by getting some&nbsp;veggies,
 that we don't get from Nuno.

00:12:35.486 --> 00:12:38.852
Maybe six or so?

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:26.321
So, after our shopping trip,
 we head into&nbsp;our storage container we finished in a&nbsp;previous update.

00:13:26.321 --> 00:13:28.049
Let me show you!

00:13:28.049 --> 00:13:32.940
here, in the&nbsp;first part, we have General Help's tools.

00:13:36.011 --> 00:13:45.003
In the back, we both have on the left&nbsp;
the workspace storage, and here's our new food&nbsp;storage.

00:13:45.003 --> 00:13:53.096
this here enables us to have mainly&nbsp;more space in the kitchen, for just working there to be honest

00:13:53.096 --> 00:13:55.245
because it was getting quite&nbsp;
tight

00:13:56.282 --> 00:14:01.527
But, it also enables us to buy bigger quantities,
 and store bigger quantities.

00:14:01.527 --> 00:14:05.591
We have some legumes, lentils, soybeans, couscous...

00:14:05.591 --> 00:14:12.088
We've got a big barrel of oats and sugar,
flax seeds, sunflower seeds, soya granulate...

00:14:12.088 --> 00:14:17.164
At some point, in this cacao barrel, 
there was about 25 kilos.

00:14:17.164 --> 00:14:20.489
Now, there's not so much left as you can see.

00:14:20.489 --> 00:14:24.130
Some washing powder that we also buy in&nbsp;bulk at Tata.

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:29.323
Some reusable packaging, like these glass jars,
 which were filling up the kitchen before.

00:14:29.323 --> 00:14:34.398
The soap we're using, or the baking soda...

00:14:34.398 --> 00:14:40.540
As you can see, the storage space gives us a lot&nbsp;
of new options to potentially reduce our carbon footprint.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:43.413
Maybe, even cater to more people!

00:14:43.413 --> 00:14:48.470
If you&nbsp;have any ideas on what we can do with it,
 please feel free to leave a comment!

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:52.222
For us, it's still&nbsp;very new.

00:14:52.222 --> 00:14:53.895
We only have it for a couple of weeks.

00:14:53.895 --> 00:15:00.600
But, for now, we leave this space,
 and we head to&nbsp;the kitchen to cook the lunch for today.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:05.293
We want to show you around the kitchen&nbsp;a bit.

00:15:05.293 --> 00:15:09.949
There's a whole video on how this kitchen
 has been built by One Army.

00:15:09.949 --> 00:15:12.360
You can check it out in the link below.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:20.577
The first thing you see when you enter&nbsp;the kitchen
 might be the queue for the washing machine.

00:15:20.577 --> 00:15:24.873
This one can be a bit noisy sometimes,
 but it's not the worst thing in Kamp.

00:15:24.873 --> 00:15:26.969
A small storage cabinet here.

00:15:26.969 --> 00:15:31.682
We have some seeds and kernels, 
and over here some nuts.

00:15:31.682 --> 00:15:35.041
A lot of the goods, like rice and pasta

00:15:35.544 --> 00:15:37.906
This is our big fridge!

00:15:39.037 --> 00:15:46.349
It has a lot of storage options,
 which is very nice, but it can get crowded sometimes.

00:15:46.664 --> 00:15:47.775
As you can see right now.

00:15:47.775 --> 00:15:51.681
A whole lot of fresh veggies in the drawers

00:15:51.681 --> 00:15:55.253
And then, usually, it's some leftover fruit.

00:15:55.253 --> 00:16:00.303
A little wine, some beer, then some salad.

00:16:00.303 --> 00:16:02.256
The pizza boxes are down there.

00:16:02.256 --> 00:16:09.840
Unfortunately, we are getting&nbsp;mold in the fridge,
 and it gets a bit humid inside...

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:14.015
We don't really have a good idea yet on how to&nbsp;tackle that.

00:16:14.015 --> 00:16:19.724
So maybe if you have something,
 please comment, or let us know somewhere!

00:16:19.724 --> 00:16:22.295
Usually, we have a drying rack here.

00:16:22.295 --> 00:16:28.263
So, when stuff is washed up, and there is usually
 quite the mountain forming&nbsp;during the cooking.

00:16:28.263 --> 00:16:30.097
Here, we have a beautiful oven.

00:16:30.097 --> 00:16:38.735
It's not working as an oven,
 because we don't&nbsp;have three-phase electricity... 
We use it for storage of some dry stuff.

00:16:38.735 --> 00:16:41.596
Like, onions, potatoes, pumpkins&nbsp;as you can see back there.

00:16:41.596 --> 00:16:43.904
Here are our beauties!

00:16:43.904 --> 00:16:48.049
All the pans and woks, and they are cast iron.

00:16:48.049 --> 00:16:53.898
We try to take&nbsp;good care of them as well like,
 when after we've used them, we put some oil in.

00:16:53.898 --> 00:16:57.515
We, of course,&nbsp;never use soap to wash them.

00:16:57.515 --> 00:17:00.917
We're cooking for almost 25 people right now.

00:17:00.917 --> 00:17:02.940
Most of it we fit.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:08.190
If shove comes to push, we have the very big one up here!

00:17:09.352 --> 00:17:14.848
Because we don't have too much time left&nbsp;until lunch,
 we should get into the cooking.

00:17:14.848 --> 00:17:16.356
Paule already started!

00:17:16.356 --> 00:17:20.228
- I think we're good in time, but&nbsp;we should start now!

00:17:20.228 --> 00:17:25.762
A vegan red thai curry, which should have some nice heat.

00:17:25.762 --> 00:17:30.665
Timo has a different&nbsp;different style of cooking than me.

00:17:30.665 --> 00:17:33.862
But, I think it fits perfectly!

00:17:33.862 --> 00:17:35.707
- It's a lot of fun together!

00:17:35.707 --> 00:17:36.590
Yep!

00:17:37.627 --> 00:17:39.600
- It doesn't feel like work much...

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:43.084
If that's what you say at least!

00:17:43.932 --> 00:17:45.728
- German joke!

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.751
Unfortunately, you want to leave me.

00:17:49.751 --> 00:17:52.339
I'm&nbsp;pretty sad about that...

00:17:52.339 --> 00:17:56.990
- It's really hard to go, 
but I also have a lot of nice things to go to, so...

00:17:57.686 --> 00:17:59.914
I don't know how the next one will be.

00:17:59.914 --> 00:18:07.116
There is a lot of people in the group also leaving and coming,
so you have to get used to a bit of change.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.175
It's okay for coconut milk, yeah?

00:19:36.175 --> 00:19:37.655
It's kind of thick!

00:19:37.655 --> 00:19:45.313
We're&nbsp;kind of making it ourselves,
with sunflower kernels, coconut oil, and water.

00:19:45.313 --> 00:19:48.900
It works quite well&nbsp;instead of buying all these coconut milk cans.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.828
So, now, we've prepared the food on the counter!

00:20:08.828 --> 00:20:11.880
It's ready for the people to load their plates!

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:16.853
Paule, do you want to sound the horn,&nbsp;
for the last meal here?

00:20:16.853 --> 00:20:18.037
- Yes! Yes, yes!

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:36.302
That means that the meal is ready!

00:20:36.302 --> 00:20:39.600
We&nbsp;also synced it to the office, with our walkie-talkie.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:45.990
Now, at some point, people will come here and eat!

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:49.260
It should be thai red curry, but it's brown.

00:21:04.003 --> 00:21:06.223
I'm late for lunch!

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:15.000
After the cooking is done, the leftovers go into&nbsp;the fridge,
 and food scraps go into the compost.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:19.840
At the moment, we use it mainly as soil&nbsp;for our kitchen garden.

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:22.658
After resting a bit, Paule will show you around.

00:21:23.852 --> 00:21:31.654
So, besides our&nbsp;suppliers we already showed you,
 we also grow a little bit of things in our little garden.

00:21:31.654 --> 00:21:38.359
Here, we got a little bit of lemongrass for teas,
 and some rosemary, which is a little&nbsp;bit burnt.

00:21:38.359 --> 00:21:39.901
But, still, it's working out!

00:21:39.901 --> 00:21:47.940
Some thyme down here, and also some wild&nbsp;mint,
 which there is a lot of.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:53.572
We also have some fruit trees!

00:21:53.572 --> 00:21:56.630
For example, this&nbsp;peach tree was planted in the first season!

00:21:56.630 --> 00:22:04.380
As you can see right now, it's full of ripe fruits,&nbsp;
and ants, but it's some really delicious fruit!

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.083
A fig tree, which already gave some nice fruits.

00:22:09.083 --> 00:22:14.720
Also, they will take some more time to be edible and ripe.

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:15.699
An avocado tree!

00:22:15.699 --> 00:22:22.309
It's quite healthy right now,&nbsp;but also it will take some years to get some fruit and some nice avocados out of that.

00:22:22.309 --> 00:22:23.705
An almond tree we planted.

00:22:23.705 --> 00:22:27.815
The same as the avocado: it's just too young to give some nuts.

00:22:27.815 --> 00:22:30.134
but it's here&nbsp;and healthy

00:22:30.134 --> 00:22:35.006
Now, I'm going to show you our little experimental garden.

00:22:35.006 --> 00:22:36.303
Just for fun!

