This came from the compost actually!

00:22:50.448 --> 00:22:52.223
For now, it's just&nbsp;a small scale.

00:22:53.731 --> 00:22:55.526
We're just experimenting and having some fun!

00:22:55.526 --> 00:23:01.235
But also, we're getting supplied by our neighbors a little bit
 for harvesting or just helping them.

00:23:01.235 --> 00:23:05.748
Here, you can see some nice&nbsp;footage of the latest potato harvest. 

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:28.048
I'm doing very well!

00:23:29.745 --> 00:23:37.560
But, no&nbsp;matter how hard I try, I always break some
and miss some, so it's really difficult.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:27.820
After helping the neighbors with the potato harvest,
we got a gift, which is a big bag of huge, nice&nbsp;potatoes!

00:25:28.763 --> 00:25:33.766
Today, as we are also heating the&nbsp;oven for baking our bread...

00:25:33.766 --> 00:25:38.585
We decided to make baked potatoes basically with some toppings!

00:25:38.585 --> 00:25:41.455
Tim was already preparing that.

00:25:41.455 --> 00:25:43.920
An onion tomato salad here.

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:47.169
Our self-made pickles&nbsp;right now...

00:25:47.169 --> 00:25:49.715
And, in the back, you can see chickpeas boiling.

00:25:49.715 --> 00:25:56.474
With that, we are going to do hummus,
&nbsp;and also maybe roast some of the chickpeas in the oven.

00:25:56.474 --> 00:25:58.386
They're still hot!

00:25:58.386 --> 00:26:00.647
Just put it&nbsp;in here.

00:26:01.841 --> 00:26:05.777
Now, it's quite smooth!

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.635
The spices will go through it later.

00:26:08.635 --> 00:26:10.782
Also, with&nbsp;some toppings.

00:26:10.782 --> 00:26:15.923
Now, we're gonna cook the potatoes,
 before putting it into the oven.

00:26:15.923 --> 00:26:19.928
Because,&nbsp;we are not really sure how high
 the temperature should be...

00:26:19.928 --> 00:26:22.590
If it's enough for these huge potatoes&nbsp;to cook.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:29.479
We also don't want to peel them,
 but cook them as a whole, to have better taste.

00:26:29.479 --> 00:26:32.716
Basically, a better consistency in the middle.

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:02.447
Now, let's see how the potatoes are doing

00:27:02.447 --> 00:27:03.984
They're still quite hard.

00:27:03.984 --> 00:27:06.515
It was expectable...

00:27:13.271 --> 00:27:16.257
A bit of salt just, and just pour some oil.

00:27:16.257 --> 00:27:17.720
They are still really hot!

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:21.333
So, now, I'm gonna take&nbsp;that to the pizza hut.

00:27:21.333 --> 00:27:25.595
As you can see, the bread team is already taking out the bread.

00:27:25.595 --> 00:27:28.252
The oven is&nbsp;still quite hot!

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:33.934
It's really hot.

00:27:33.934 --> 00:27:36.381
about 300 degrees in the back.

00:27:36.381 --> 00:27:38.028
250&nbsp;in the middle, which is perfect.

00:27:39.222 --> 00:27:41.411
We will do with another batch too!

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:52.759
Now, we are going to check,
 if the potatoes are&nbsp;already done.

00:27:52.759 --> 00:27:55.080
We have the second batch also in.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:57.533
Let's show it to you!

00:27:57.533 --> 00:28:02.481
That's basically how it should be, I guess?

00:28:02.481 --> 00:28:03.678
It is done!

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:29.640
- It's food time!

00:28:56.580 --> 00:29:06.112
So, now you've&nbsp;seen what we do on a daily basis in
and&nbsp;around the kitchen, here at Project Kamp.

00:29:06.112 --> 00:29:11.113
But,&nbsp;we're also trying to keep on researching,
 and improve further on.

00:29:11.113 --> 00:29:13.152
We're looking a bit more into&nbsp;the data.

00:29:13.152 --> 00:29:14.760
I can show you some things here.

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.742
So, here you can see our receipt data in detail

00:29:18.742 --> 00:29:21.858
Don't worry -- I'll try not to bore you with all these numbers.

00:29:21.858 --> 00:29:23.066
Just to give you an idea:

00:29:23.066 --> 00:29:30.481
We gathered&nbsp;all the food and corresponding prices amounts,
and CO2-equivalents in a time frame of about 11&nbsp;weeks.

00:29:30.481 --> 00:29:35.950
In this 11-week time frame, we tracked more than 1.1 tons of food.

00:29:35.950 --> 00:29:41.196
The colorful pie chart shows how much variety we have
 in terms of the ingredients we're using.

00:29:41.887 --> 00:29:46.891
After matching the food we&nbsp;consumed 
with the environmental impact data from two studies...

00:29:46.891 --> 00:29:51.745
We arrived at a total about 2.8 tons&nbsp;
of CO2-equivalence we emitted.

00:29:51.745 --> 00:29:56.942
It was interesting to see that although we only
 consumed 17&nbsp;kilograms of coffee...

00:29:56.942 --> 00:30:01.366
It is by far the biggest slice of the emissions pie at 17%.

00:30:01.366 --> 00:30:08.793
We also&nbsp;found out that our trips to the shops and the farms
 are a considerable part of our emissions around 7%.

00:30:08.793 --> 00:30:16.389
With the emissions of all the 1.1 tons of food,
 we got we could have gotten&nbsp;only 28 kilograms of beef!

00:30:16.389 --> 00:30:21.868
It has a comparable environmental impact,
 but it's almost 40 times less&nbsp;in volume.

00:30:21.868 --> 00:30:25.120
I also doubt we would have been able 
to keep the group fed with it!

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:29.440
Even though there are&nbsp;still limitations of 
our research and the data we used...

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:35.463
It's nice seeing that our projected&nbsp;yearly emissions
 are almost three times less than the average person!

00:30:35.463 --> 00:30:37.609
But, there is always room to improve.

00:30:37.609 --> 00:30:43.287
So, one challenge we're facing right now,
 after having done this research:

00:30:43.287 --> 00:30:47.481
What&nbsp;do we do with the resource intensive goods we are consuming?

00:30:47.481 --> 00:30:50.391
This is mostly coffee and cheese.

00:30:50.391 --> 00:30:57.536
It's&nbsp;kind of a sensitive topic to many people, 
if you take away the the coffee, for example in the&nbsp;mornings.

00:30:57.536 --> 00:30:59.783
I'm not sure if that would work out so well...

00:30:59.783 --> 00:31:01.849
